item.description:
These are not real HTTP requests — they are reference examples showing config JSON you can write directly to the `newspaper_templates.config` column to use the new dynamic resolver features.

Update a template's config via the Supabase dashboard SQL editor:
  UPDATE newspaper_templates SET config = '<json>'::jsonb WHERE id = '<template_id>';

request.description:
Example config with all dynamic resolver features enabled.

Paste this into `newspaper_templates.config` to test everything at once:

```json
{
  "title": "Daily Brief",
  "date": "today",
  "theme": "classic",
  "paper_size": "A4",
  "orientation": "portrait",
  "google_calendar_sources": [
    {
      "account_id": "<google_accounts.id>",
      "calendar_names": ["Work", "Personal"]
    }
  ],
  "pages": [
    {
      "layout": "two-column",
      "columns": [
        [
          {
            "type": "title",
            "text": "Daily Brief",
            "date_format": "EEEE, MMMM d, yyyy",
            "style": "newspaper",
            "border": "both"
          },
          {
            "type": "calendar-day",
            "date": "today",
            "hours": [8, 20],
            "use_global_entries": true
          },
          {
            "type": "todos",
            "title": "Today's Tasks",
            "query": {
              "filter": "due_today"
            },
            "show_priority": true,
            "show_project": true
          },
          {
            "type": "todos",
            "title": "Overdue",
            "query": {
              "filter": "overdue",
              "limit": 5
            },
            "show_priority": true
          }
        ],
        [
          {
            "type": "weather",
            "location": "Berlin",
            "unit": "celsius",
            "display": "forecast-3"
          },
          {
            "type": "calendar-week",
            "start_date": "week-start",
            "use_global_entries": true,
            "show_week_number": true
          },
          {
            "type": "articles",
            "title": "Reading List",
            "query": {
              "filter": "unread",
              "limit": 8
            },
            "show_site": true,
            "show_excerpt": false
          }
        ]
      ]
    }
  ]
}
```

Instead of specifying Google Calendar IDs (opaque strings), you can now use human-readable calendar names.

**Before (calendar IDs required):**
```json
{
  "google_calendar_sources": [
    {
      "account_id": "<uuid>",
      "calendar_ids": ["work@group.calendar.google.com", "personal@gmail.com"]
    }
  ]
}
```

**After (names resolved automatically):**
```json
{
  "google_calendar_sources": [
    {
      "account_id": "<uuid>",
      "calendar_names": ["Work", "Personal"]
    }
  ]
}
```

**You can also mix both:**
```json
{
  "google_calendar_sources": [
    {
      "account_id": "<uuid>",
      "calendar_ids": ["known-calendar-id"],
      "calendar_names": ["Another Calendar"]
    }
  ]
}
```

Names are matched case-insensitively. Unknown names are silently skipped.
